<header class="personal-page-head"><p class="small-label">The person behind the notebook</p><h1 class="page-title">Hi, I’m Vansh.<br/><em>I get curious.</em></h1><p class="intro-deck">Usually about aircraft, manufacturing, or something my computer is doing. Sometimes about all three.</p></header>
<div class="about-facts"><span>Hofstra University</span><span>Mechanical engineering · Aerospace</span><span>Class of 2027</span><span>Long Island, NY</span></div>
<div class="personal-about-grid">
<div class="prose">
<section id="background"><h2>Engineering is how I follow the question.</h2><p>I’m a mechanical engineering student concentrating in aerospace at Hofstra University. Aircraft design, rocketry, and simulation are the subjects I keep coming back to. I like taking a question far enough that I can model it, build something around it, or work out a way to test it.</p><p>I’m also co-president of Hofstra’s AIAA chapter. Projects, workshops, and connections outside class are part of how I want to make aerospace more accessible to other students.</p></section>
<section id="experience"><h2>The drawing is only part of the job.</h2><p>I spent six months as an Aerospace Manufacturing Engineer Co-op at Arkwin Industries, working with hydraulic actuators and the tooling used to manufacture them. My work included inspection fixtures, CNC workholding, custom cutting tools, engineering drawings, and manufacturing documentation.</p><p>That made tolerances, inspection, and clear instructions feel much less abstract. A design has to make sense to the person making the part and the person checking it.</p><blockquote>A model becomes much more interesting when you have to work out how someone will make and measure it.</blockquote><p>At Hofstra’s ideaHUb makerspace, I worked as a research intern supporting prototyping, mechanical testing, troubleshooting, and equipment guides. Other projects have included a low-cost below-knee prosthetic leg and adapting children’s toys for users with limited mobility.</p></section>
<section id="computing"><h2>Then there’s the computer rabbit hole.</h2><p>I use Fedora Linux, and I care about how a desktop feels to use: performance, usability, repairability, and control. A small annoyance can turn into comparing alternatives, reading about the architecture, or trying to build a tool of my own.</p><p>Python and MATLAB are part of my engineering work. Hobby projects also give me a reason to explore Rust and TypeScript. I use AI tools in that process, but getting an answer or some running code is only the beginning. I still need to understand the assumptions and check the behavior.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('projects/')}>Explore the software and engineering projects →</a></section>
<section id="outside"><h2>Beyond coursework.</h2><p>I’m into cars, especially rotary engines. One dream build is a late-1990s or early-2000s Honda NSX with a five-rotor, bridgeported, twin-turbo billet rotary and a sequential gearbox. Very much a dream build, but a good indication of the sort of engineering rabbit hole I enjoy.</p><p>I also spend time with modded Minecraft, new technology, and the question of why software performs the way it does. Smooth frame times can be just as interesting a troubleshooting problem as a desktop that refuses to behave.</p></section>
<section class="working-principles"><p class="small-label">How I want to approach the work</p><h2>Leave a trail someone else can check.</h2><dl><div><dt>Start with the conditions.</dt><dd>A comparison needs the same mission, inputs, or workload before the numbers mean much.</dd></div><div><dt>Keep assumptions visible.</dt><dd>A chosen structural penalty or planned tunnel setup should not look like a measurement.</dd></div><div><dt>Save what explains the result.</dt><dd>Methods, settings, and limitations belong alongside the plot or the conclusion.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section id="notebook"><h2>Why keep this site?</h2><p>I want somewhere to explain what I’m trying, not just list what I’ve done. The project pages hold the scope and current state of the work. The blog is where I can spend more time on a decision, an experiment, or something I’m learning.</p><p>A proposal stays a proposal until there are results. When something doesn’t work, that belongs in the write-up too.</p></section>
</div>
